<DocsLayout title="First steps with Haft v9" description="Initialize one host, restart it onto the v9 binary, check onboarding, and start from the current engineering question.">
  <h1>First steps</h1>

  <h2>1. Initialize the host you use</h2>

  <pre><code>cd /path/to/project
haft init                    # interactive host selection, TTY only
haft init --claude           # Claude MCP, skills, and managed CLAUDE.md section
haft init --codex            # Codex MCP, skills, and managed AGENTS.md section
haft init --core-only        # project core without host publication</code></pre>

  <p>
    Scripts and CI must pass explicit flags. Bare non-interactive
    <code>haft init</code> fails before writing instead of guessing a host.
    Re-init replaces recognized legacy Haft skills and changes only the marked
    Haft section of a project instruction file.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Restart and verify the loaded runtime</h2>

  <pre><code>haft version
haft doctor</code></pre>

  <p>
    Restart the coding agent after init or upgrade. A running MCP process keeps
    the binary and skill catalog it loaded at startup. Command output from the
    source checkout does not prove which binary the host process is using.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Check onboarding state</h2>

  <p>
    Ask the host agent for <code>/h-onboard</code> or <code>/h-status</code> when
    setup state is the current question. Onboarding reports one readable state:
    <code>needs_init</code>, <code>needs_profile</code>,
    <code>profile_review_ready</code>,
    <code>profile_change_review_ready</code>, or <code>ready</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>haft init</code> installs default project memory automatically. A
    complete supported singleton profile may be admitted as
    <code>detector_default</code>. Ambiguous or reviewed bases remain an
    operator choice prepared through the onboarding surface.
  </p>

  <div class="callout-warn">
    <p>
      <strong>Ready is not a global green light.</strong> It means only that the
      canonical profile and structured project memory are available. Check
      specification health, evidence, drift, and release readiness separately.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>4. Start from the current question</h2>

  <pre><code>/h-reason We need to change the API cache. What is the actual
problem, and which FPF source is relevant?</code></pre>

  <p>
    Use <code>h-reason</code> when the question is ambiguous or crosses several
    capabilities. When the result type is already known, use the narrower
    skill directly:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><code>/h-frame</code> for an under-articulated problem;</li>
    <li><code>/h-diagnose</code> for a concrete failure with rival causes;</li>
    <li><code>/h-explore</code> for distinct approaches;</li>
    <li><code>/h-compare</code> for a parity-based comparison;</li>
    <li><code>/h-verify</code> for a recorded claim against current evidence;</li>
    <li><code>/h-spec</code> for specification carriers and lifecycle;</li>
    <li><code>/h-status</code> for the read-only project cockpit.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    This list is not a required order. A simple reversible edit with clear
    acceptance can proceed without an FPF query or durable artifacts.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Persist only what later work needs</h2>

  <p>
    Reasoning can remain in the conversation. Use <code>/h-note</code> when you
    explicitly want one non-binding observation saved. A binding choice needs
    one exact operator request routed through <code>h-decide</code>. Execution
    authority needs the manual-only <code>h-commission</code> boundary.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Use the right read surface</h2>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Question</th>
        <th>Surface</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>What needs attention?</td><td><code>/h-status</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Which FPF source unit is relevant?</td><td><code>haft fpf query|lookup|inspect</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Where is this behavior implemented?</td><td><code>haft graph explore</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>What is known around this project object?</td><td><code>haft memory resolve|neighborhood|recall</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Are specification carriers structurally healthy?</td><td><code>haft spec check</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Should CI fail on current governance findings?</td><td><code>haft check</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>7. Share carriers through git</h2>

  <p>
    Commit reviewable <code>.haft/*.md</code> carriers with the code they
    explain. After pulling, teammates run <code>haft sync</code> to import those
    carriers into their local SQLite store. Markdown is the exchange carrier;
    SQLite remains the runtime structured store.
  </p>

